      "text": "We are looking for a unique two-digit integer \\( t \\) such that the last two digits of \\( 11t \\) are 36. This can be expressed as the congruence:\n\\[\n11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\nOur goal is to solve for \\( t \\). First, we will use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100. We need to find an integer \\( x \\) such that:\n\\[\n11x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nTo apply the Extended Euclidean Algorithm, we start with the Euclidean algorithm steps:\n\\[\n100 = 9 \\cdot 11 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n1 = 100 - 9 \\cdot 11\n\\]\nThis tells us that:\n\\[\n1 = 100 - 9 \\cdot 11\n\\]\nThus, the coefficient of 11, which is \\(-9\\), is the inverse of 11 modulo 100. To convert it to a positive equivalent, we take:\n\\[\n-9 \\equiv 91 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\nHence, the inverse of 11 modulo 100 is 91.\n\nNext, we multiply both sides of the original congruence by 91 to isolate \\( t \\):\n\\[\nt \\equiv 36 \\cdot 91 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\nNow, we calculate \\( 36 \\cdot 91 \\):\n\\[\n36 \\cdot 91 = 3276\n\\]\nNext, we find the last two digits of 3276:\n\\[\n3276 \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\nThus, the solution is:\n\\[\nt \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\nSince \\( t \\) is a two-digit integer, we have:\n\\[\nt = 76\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) for which the last two digits of \\( 11t \\) are 36 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{76}\n\\]",
