      "text": "To find the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) such that the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot t \\) are 36, we need to solve the congruence:\n\n\\[ 11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100} \\]\n\nFirst, we need to find the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100. This is a number \\( x \\) such that:\n\n\\[ 11x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100} \\]\n\nWe can use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find this inverse. We start by applying the Euclidean algorithm to 100 and 11:\n\n\\[\n100 = 9 \\cdot 11 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n11 = 11 \\cdot 1 + 0\n\\]\n\nFrom the first equation, we can express 1 as:\n\n\\[\n1 = 100 - 9 \\cdot 11\n\\]\n\nThis shows that the inverse of 11 modulo 100 is \\(-9\\). Since we want a positive inverse, we convert \\(-9\\) to a positive equivalent by adding 100:\n\n\\[\n-9 + 100 = 91\n\\]\n\nThus, the inverse of 11 modulo 100 is 91. Now we can solve the original congruence by multiplying both sides by 91:\n\n\\[ t \\equiv 36 \\cdot 91 \\pmod{100} \\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\( 36 \\cdot 91 \\):\n\n\\[\n36 \\cdot 91 = 3276\n\\]\n\nNow we find the remainder when 3276 is divided by 100:\n\n\\[\n3276 \\div 100 = 32 \\text{ remainder } 76\n\\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\n3276 \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( t \\) is:\n\n\\[\nt = 76\n\\]\n\nWe can verify that the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot 76 \\) are indeed 36:\n\n\\[\n11 \\cdot 76 = 836\n\\]\n\nThe last two digits of 836 are 36, confirming our solution. Therefore, the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{76}\n\\]",
