      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) such that the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot t \\) are 36, we need to solve the congruence:\n\\[\n11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to find the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100. That is, we need an integer \\( x \\) such that:\n\\[\n11x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nTo find \\( x \\), we use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to solve the equation:\n\\[\n100 = 9 \\cdot 11 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n1 = 100 - 9 \\cdot 11\n\\]\n\nThis shows that:\n\\[\n-9 \\cdot 11 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\nThus, the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100 is \\( -9 \\). Since we need a positive inverse, we convert \\( -9 \\) to a positive equivalent by adding 100:\n\\[\n-9 + 100 = 91\n\\]\n\nSo, the inverse of 11 modulo 100 is \\( 91 \\).\n\nNext, we use this inverse to solve the original congruence:\n\\[\n11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\nMultiplying both sides by 91, we get:\n\\[\nt \\equiv 36 \\cdot 91 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate \\( 36 \\cdot 91 \\):\n\\[\n36 \\cdot 91 = 3276\n\\]\n\nTo find the last two digits of 3276, we compute:\n\\[\n3276 \\mod 100 = 76\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\nt \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( t \\) is a two-digit positive integer, the only solution is:\n\\[\nt = 76\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) for which the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot t \\) are 36 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{76}\n\\]",
